1
00:00:02,003 --> 00:00:03,004
Ho-ho!

2
00:00:03,088 --> 00:00:05,256
Look who's back reporting for duty.
Hey, guys.

3
00:00:06,091 --> 00:00:07,258
Hey.
Hi.

4
00:00:07,509 --> 00:00:08,927
Pam! Ha!

5
00:00:09,010 --> 00:00:10,887
Look what I can do now that
she's no longer pregnant!

6
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:12,347
Bam! Bam! Bam! Pam!

7
00:00:12,722 --> 00:00:13,932
Are you glad
to be back?

8
00:00:14,265 --> 00:00:17,060
Yeah. Yeah. I mean, I could have
used another week, or three.

9
00:00:17,143 --> 00:00:19,270
You still had eight
weeks more than we did.

10
00:00:19,437 --> 00:00:21,773
Well, it's not
exactly a vacation.

11
00:00:21,856 --> 00:00:25,318
Hello, everyone! Remember?
Little old me. Hi.

12
00:00:25,402 --> 00:00:27,237
What are you doing here?
You just had our baby.

13
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:32,617
Our collective Dunder Mifflin
family baby four days ago.

14
00:00:32,951 --> 00:00:34,202
I wanted to come
back to work.

15
00:00:34,285 --> 00:00:38,581
Not everybody needs some long,
luxurious, Parisian maternity leave.

16
00:00:39,124 --> 00:00:41,000
Damn, girl! Your body!
What?

17
00:00:41,209 --> 00:00:42,877
Angela, you
look amazing.

18
00:00:43,253 --> 00:00:46,506
Really? I feel like
this big rhinoceros.

19
00:00:47,173 --> 00:00:50,468
Anyway, I wanted to thank you guys for
covering for me, so I made some treats.

20
00:00:50,552 --> 00:00:52,387
I've got brownies
and magic cookie bars.

21
00:00:52,470 --> 00:00:53,972
Mm-mm-mm.

22
00:00:54,848 --> 00:00:58,685
Angela, those brownies have walnuts in them
and I think Kevin's allergic to walnuts.

23
00:00:58,768 --> 00:01:00,311
You're allergic to
walnuts, right, Kevin?

24
00:01:00,562 --> 00:01:02,647
Extremely, but I'm going
to fight through it.

25
00:01:03,356 --> 00:01:05,692
It's okay, Angela.
I have mommy brain, too.

26
00:01:05,984 --> 00:01:07,444
I don't know
what that is, Pam.

27
00:01:07,527 --> 00:01:10,155
I made a batch with no nuts, special
for you, Kevin. Right here.

28
00:01:10,697 --> 00:01:12,031
When did you find time
to do all this?

29
00:01:12,115 --> 00:01:14,325
Babies sleep a lot, Pam,
if you feed them enough.

30
00:01:15,368 --> 00:01:16,995
Brownie, Pam?

31
00:01:21,833 --> 00:01:23,209
Thank you.

32
00:01:25,086 --> 00:01:27,172
Oh, my gosh.
It's really good.

33
00:01:27,505 --> 00:01:29,549
I wouldn't know.
I'm watching my weight.

34
00:01:33,261 --> 00:01:36,765
Say, guys, you know what's an even
more useful treat is cash. So...

35
00:01:36,890 --> 00:01:39,684
Nope. It's over. No. Okay. We
wanted to say thank you...

36
00:01:49,235 --> 00:01:51,362
Dwight, I have exciting
news for you. What?

37
00:01:52,947 --> 00:01:54,240
I think you're
going to like it.

38
00:01:54,324 --> 00:01:55,658
You can't tantalize me.

39
00:01:55,742 --> 00:01:58,036
Oh, maybe I can.

40
00:01:58,119 --> 00:02:00,288
I got an e-mail
from corporate

41
00:02:00,371 --> 00:02:02,415
that told me...
Okay.

42
00:02:02,791 --> 00:02:04,459
...that you got
a promotion!

43
00:02:05,877 --> 00:02:08,838
Right? I mean, that's not the kind of thing
you want to read quietly at your desk.

44
00:02:08,922 --> 00:02:11,257
If you make me head of sales
one more time, I swear.

45
00:02:11,341 --> 00:02:12,342
No. This is legit.

46
00:02:12,425 --> 00:02:16,137
You will be assembling a team to
go to Tallahassee for three weeks

47
00:02:16,221 --> 00:02:19,724
to develop and launch
a chain of Sabre stores.

48
00:02:20,517 --> 00:02:23,269
Yes! Yes! Yes! Yes!

49
00:02:23,561 --> 00:02:25,563
Yes! Yes! Yes!

50
00:02:25,772 --> 00:02:29,567
Yes! Yes! Yes! Yes! Yes!

51
00:02:29,901 --> 00:02:31,444
Yeah! Yeah! Yeah!
Yeah! Yeah!

52
00:02:31,569 --> 00:02:32,821
The Schrutes
have a word for when

53
00:02:32,904 --> 00:02:35,990
everything in a man's life
comes together perfectly.

54
00:02:36,074 --> 00:02:37,992
"Perfektenschlag."

55
00:02:38,076 --> 00:02:39,828
Right now, I'm in it.

56
00:02:39,911 --> 00:02:42,121
I finally get a chance to
prove myself to corporate.

57
00:02:42,205 --> 00:02:45,416
I am assembling a competent team.
I am likely a father.

58
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:50,380
I am so deep inside of
perfektenschlag right now.

59
00:02:50,463 --> 00:02:53,341
And just to be clear,
there is a second definition,

60
00:02:53,424 --> 00:02:55,844
"perfect pork anus,"
which I don't mean.

61
00:02:56,219 --> 00:02:58,221
And here's
the fax for you.

62
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:00,515
And here's
some facts for you.

63
00:03:00,598 --> 00:03:03,101
Did you know frogs can
hear with their lungs?

64
00:03:03,935 --> 00:03:06,938
And that flamingos can have
orgasms that last 30 minutes.

65
00:03:07,897 --> 00:03:10,775
Love to learn. Yeah, it's weird
that I said that last one.

66
00:03:11,192 --> 00:03:12,610
So...

67
00:03:12,902 --> 00:03:14,863
Yeah, look at that.
We're pin twins.

68
00:03:14,946 --> 00:03:16,281
Pin twins.

69
00:03:16,364 --> 00:03:18,867
This is a gift from Jessica.
Kind of a gag gift, really.

70
00:03:18,950 --> 00:03:19,993
Real gifts come later.

71
00:03:20,368 --> 00:03:22,328
Yeah. That's cute.

72
00:03:22,412 --> 00:03:25,081
When I saw it at CVS,
it made me gag, too. Just...

73
00:03:31,504 --> 00:03:34,549
For a while there, I thought
something would change,but nope.

74
00:03:34,632 --> 00:03:35,884
Andy's still with Jessica.

75
00:03:35,967 --> 00:03:38,052
They even carpool
together most days.

76
00:03:38,511 --> 00:03:39,512
I don't know.

77
00:03:39,596 --> 00:03:42,557
I wouldn't mind carpooling
every day with Andy,

78
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:45,268
but I wouldn't want to spend
that much time with Jessica.

79
00:03:46,728 --> 00:03:48,938
What do we have here?

80
00:03:53,651 --> 00:03:54,819
Val knit me a beanie.

81
00:03:55,278 --> 00:03:56,988
But I can't tell if it's a
"We're just friends" beanie,

82
00:03:57,071 --> 00:04:00,033
or a "I'm hot, you're hot,
let's get it popping" beanie.

83
00:04:00,116 --> 00:04:02,952
So I'm going
to up the ante.

84
00:04:03,036 --> 00:04:05,663
Give her a clearly
romantic gift.

85
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:08,333
And we'll get to the
meaning of the beanie.

86
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,085
Pam, pack up your
post-natal swimwear,

87
00:04:11,169 --> 00:04:12,795
make it a one piece or
this offer is rescinded,

88
00:04:12,879 --> 00:04:16,758
and join me for a fantastic barbeque one
week from today in Tallahassee, Florida,

89
00:04:16,841 --> 00:04:18,635
where I'm going to be living
for the next three weeks.

90
00:04:19,052 --> 00:04:20,595
Really?
Mm-hmm.

91
00:04:20,678 --> 00:04:22,430
I'll fly anywhere
for some good barbeque.

92
00:04:22,513 --> 00:04:23,514
Ooh, me three.

93
00:04:23,598 --> 00:04:27,518
No plus ones.
This is for competent workers only.

94
00:04:27,602 --> 00:04:30,146
And don't worry about the cost.
Sabre is footing the bill.

95
00:04:30,229 --> 00:04:32,607
Does this have anything to do with
what you were talking to Andy about?

96
00:04:32,690 --> 00:04:34,067
God, you're
such a spy.

97
00:04:37,612 --> 00:04:39,697
Notice anything
different about me?

98
00:04:39,781 --> 00:04:41,824
You're wearing the beanie.
You like it?

99
00:04:41,908 --> 00:04:44,661
Super comfortable. Like
sweatpants for my head.

100
00:04:44,744 --> 00:04:46,412
I love it too, Val.

101
00:04:46,496 --> 00:04:47,705
It's really itchy,

102
00:04:48,081 --> 00:04:51,376
uh, but to be fair, my head
is constantly itching,

103
00:04:51,459 --> 00:04:54,045
so I can't really
peg it on the hat.

104
00:04:55,171 --> 00:04:58,216
Wow. It's like the Nation
of Islam down here.

105
00:04:58,466 --> 00:05:00,802
I like to knit,
don't hate.

106
00:05:01,469 --> 00:05:03,388
Who's that for?

107
00:05:03,471 --> 00:05:08,142
Oh. This is a gift for
my man Nate here.

108
00:05:08,226 --> 00:05:09,435
What?
Yeah.

109
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:10,853
Darryl, you
shouldn't have.

110
00:05:11,896 --> 00:05:14,482
Can I open it? No,no,no,
maybe just later.

111
00:05:14,565 --> 00:05:16,734
Ah, I can't wait. I'm sorry.
I'm too excited.

112
00:05:21,447 --> 00:05:22,657
Darryl.

113
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:24,534
Wow. Those
are really nice.

114
00:05:24,617 --> 00:05:26,285
They're so elegant.

115
00:05:26,411 --> 00:05:28,246
Cashmere.
How did you know?

116
00:05:28,496 --> 00:05:30,248
Just a hunch.

117
00:05:31,457 --> 00:05:32,750
Your gift is on the way.

118
00:05:32,834 --> 00:05:34,085
Can't wait.

119
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:38,339
"I'm glad you're in my life.
Happy Valentine's Day."

120
00:05:38,423 --> 00:05:42,093
Darryl, I am glad
to be in your life, too.

121
00:05:43,261 --> 00:05:46,264
Your card is more
beautiful than the gloves.

122
00:05:48,933 --> 00:05:52,020
My first task as special
project manager Dwight Schrute,

123
00:05:52,103 --> 00:05:53,980
assembling a crack team.

124
00:05:54,897 --> 00:05:56,816
I need people who are loyal,

125
00:05:57,108 --> 00:06:00,945
people who will help me get an inroad
with the gay Hispanic community.

126
00:06:01,112 --> 00:06:03,197
People who won't be missed.

127
00:06:04,365 --> 00:06:06,325
We don't need idiots.

128
00:06:07,493 --> 00:06:09,037
Good for nothings.

129
00:06:09,704 --> 00:06:10,955
Meth heads.

130
00:06:12,290 --> 00:06:13,458
Or...

131
00:06:13,916 --> 00:06:14,959
What's your name?

132
00:06:15,126 --> 00:06:17,170
Cathy.
Cathy.

133
00:06:23,134 --> 00:06:24,844
I just got a text from
Robert California that says,

134
00:06:24,927 --> 00:06:26,471
"Bring your clubs
to Florida."

135
00:06:26,763 --> 00:06:28,806
Why? Does he think you're
going to Florida?

136
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:31,517
I hope not. Because
I am not going.

137
00:06:32,602 --> 00:06:33,728
Two question marks?

138
00:06:33,811 --> 00:06:37,398
No, just do one. Two question
marks is kind of aggressive.

139
00:06:37,482 --> 00:06:39,275
You know, it's like,
"Well, what?"

140
00:06:39,567 --> 00:06:41,277
Just do one.

141
00:06:42,028 --> 00:06:44,989
You want to take Darryl,
Phyllis, Toby, Angela and Oscar?

142
00:06:45,073 --> 00:06:46,074
Yes.

143
00:06:46,157 --> 00:06:48,618
Can I interest you
in someone less essential?

144
00:06:48,701 --> 00:06:50,995
Like a Creed or a
Meredith, perhaps?

145
00:06:51,079 --> 00:06:53,164
Oh, my God. They're not
expendable exactly.

146
00:06:53,247 --> 00:06:55,750
I just can't... I can't think
of the word I'm trying to find.

147
00:06:55,833 --> 00:06:57,001
Are you kidding me?

148
00:06:57,085 --> 00:06:58,961
I'm supposed to get on an
airplane with those nincompoops?

149
00:06:59,045 --> 00:07:00,129
We'd never make it
off the runway!

150
00:07:00,213 --> 00:07:03,633
All right, look, I got to keep
this office functioning somehow,

151
00:07:03,716 --> 00:07:06,385
so I have put
together a list.

152
00:07:06,469 --> 00:07:07,929
Here's your team.

153
00:07:08,012 --> 00:07:09,555
Darryl and Phyllis,
you can have.

154
00:07:10,098 --> 00:07:12,016
You're also taking Cathy,
Kelly and Kevin.

155
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:14,185
Oh, God.
You have your team.

156
00:07:14,435 --> 00:07:17,688
Kevin? Kelly? Cathy?

157
00:07:17,772 --> 00:07:20,149
Andy just gave me a chain
with three weak links.

158
00:07:20,233 --> 00:07:23,319
Have you ever tried to use a chain
with three weak links? I have.

159
00:07:23,402 --> 00:07:25,863
And now I no longer
own an Arctic Wolf.

160
00:07:27,949 --> 00:07:29,158
Well, let's hear it.

161
00:07:29,200 --> 00:07:31,953
"Robert, hey," exclamation point.
I like it so far.

162
00:07:32,036 --> 00:07:33,621
"Got your text.
Awesome idea.

163
00:07:33,704 --> 00:07:37,083
"Let's hit the links the next
time you're in PA. -J.H."

164
00:07:38,292 --> 00:07:42,046
It's perfect. You emphasize the
golf, completely omit the Florida.

165
00:07:42,213 --> 00:07:43,381
Yeah, it's a golf text.

166
00:07:43,673 --> 00:07:45,424
Total golf text. Send it.

167
00:07:46,175 --> 00:07:50,471
Done. Wait, I just feel weird.
I should just call him.

168
00:07:50,555 --> 00:07:54,642
You want to call someone that texted you?
Do you want to drive them away? I mean...

169
00:07:58,396 --> 00:07:59,605
This is Darryl.

170
00:07:59,689 --> 00:08:01,983
Sorry, I asked for
the main shipment number.

171
00:08:02,066 --> 00:08:03,609
I just need the address
for the warehouse.

172
00:08:03,693 --> 00:08:05,278
I want to send my
girlfriend some flowers.

173
00:08:06,195 --> 00:08:09,073
Your girlfriend?
ls your girlfriend Val?

174
00:08:09,157 --> 00:08:11,075
Yeah. Do you know her?

175
00:08:11,534 --> 00:08:15,163
We're friends.
We're friends.

176
00:08:16,164 --> 00:08:18,833
Cool. So can I get
that address or...

177
00:08:19,375 --> 00:08:22,170
Yeah, yeah. Sorry, I'm
just looking for a pen.

178
00:08:22,253 --> 00:08:23,296
Why do you
need a pen?

179
00:08:23,379 --> 00:08:25,214
Back off.
I got my reasons.

180
00:08:26,048 --> 00:08:27,175
May I have your
attention, please?

181
00:08:27,258 --> 00:08:29,010
Could Cathy, Phyllis,
Darryl, Kevin and Kelly

182
00:08:29,093 --> 00:08:31,095
please join me in the
conference room immediately?

183
00:08:31,304 --> 00:08:32,638
Why?
No big deal.

184
00:08:32,722 --> 00:08:35,933
It's just that you five have been
deemed most deserving of a three-week,

185
00:08:36,017 --> 00:08:38,436
all expenses paid business
trip to Tallahassee, Florida.

186
00:08:38,769 --> 00:08:40,563
Hell, yeah.
What?

187
00:08:40,646 --> 00:08:43,858
I'm sorry, Stanley. I can't share
any more details with you.

188
00:08:43,941 --> 00:08:46,027
You see,
Andy rejected you.

189
00:08:46,110 --> 00:08:48,112
Wait,
Cathy gets to go?

190
00:08:48,196 --> 00:08:50,406
Why does she even still work here?
Pam is back.

191
00:08:50,489 --> 00:08:51,866
It just feels like
a slap in the face.

192
00:08:51,949 --> 00:08:53,701
I can understand your pain.
And your rage.

193
00:08:53,784 --> 00:08:55,953
But you know what?
Andy is an honorable man.

194
00:08:56,037 --> 00:08:59,248
Let us not question his choices.
I'm sure he had his reasons.

195
00:08:59,332 --> 00:09:01,792
We're not questioning his reasons.
I just want to know what they are.

196
00:09:01,918 --> 00:09:03,127
I know. You and me
both, brother.

197
00:09:03,211 --> 00:09:04,378
It seemed kind
of random to me,

198
00:09:04,462 --> 00:09:08,341
but he was pretty clear on who he thought
truly deserved this boondoggle of a lifetime.

199
00:09:08,424 --> 00:09:13,095
Well, he nailed it, because
I do deserve a vacation.

200
00:09:13,179 --> 00:09:15,556
Sometimes Batman's
got to take off his cape.

201
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:18,851
Good point. But we're going to be
working pretty hard until 5:00 p.m.

202
00:09:18,935 --> 00:09:22,813
After that, it's going to be beach
volleyball, trips to Cape Canaveral

203
00:09:22,897 --> 00:09:24,815
and sea kayaking
with Gloria Estefan.

204
00:09:25,024 --> 00:09:26,567
What were the
criteria for going?

205
00:09:26,651 --> 00:09:29,987
It might be innate goodness
versus innate badness.

206
00:09:30,279 --> 00:09:32,156
But there's an easy
way to find out.

207
00:09:33,991 --> 00:09:36,994
Oh, Andy, got a few
questions for you.

208
00:09:37,870 --> 00:09:39,664
<i>Moshi moshi</i>

209
00:09:40,456 --> 00:09:42,375
Uh, the Florida
picks got out,

210
00:09:42,458 --> 00:09:45,419
and people were just kind of curious
as to why they weren't picked,

211
00:09:45,503 --> 00:09:47,421
and maybe you
could just clarify.

212
00:09:47,505 --> 00:09:48,506
Uh...

213
00:09:49,924 --> 00:09:55,096
Well, the deliberations were confidential,
so I feel like we should respect that.

214
00:09:55,179 --> 00:09:57,056
Respect it? You're
trying to tell me

215
00:09:57,139 --> 00:09:59,350
Kevin Malone deserves more
than Stanley Hudson?

216
00:09:59,809 --> 00:10:01,602
Hey.
Okay, it's obvious

217
00:10:01,686 --> 00:10:04,939
that Andy picked the people that
are least important to the office.

218
00:10:05,022 --> 00:10:07,608
Important? Because you and
your enormous monster baby

219
00:10:07,692 --> 00:10:08,776
are so important
to the branch?

220
00:10:08,859 --> 00:10:10,695
My baby is
not a monster.

221
00:10:10,778 --> 00:10:13,155
Hey, be proud of your
enormous monster baby.

222
00:10:13,239 --> 00:10:14,949
I was once an enormous
monster baby.

223
00:10:15,032 --> 00:10:18,327
Guys! Guys! Guys!

224
00:10:18,577 --> 00:10:23,416
Come on, seriously, we cannot let this
trip cause this kind of rift, guys.

225
00:10:23,499 --> 00:10:24,792
Stop calling us "guys"!

226
00:10:24,959 --> 00:10:28,879
I use the word "guys" a lot
when I'm nervous, guys.

227
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:31,173
- Guys, guys, guys. Guys.
- Stop it.

228
00:10:31,257 --> 00:10:33,134
Stop! Stop it! Andy.

229
00:10:34,010 --> 00:10:35,594
Dudes.

230
00:10:35,678 --> 00:10:39,974
The dudes who are going to Florida
were picked by Dwight and me.

231
00:10:40,057 --> 00:10:41,976
Okay, hey, I
specifically remember

232
00:10:42,059 --> 00:10:44,270
that there were a lot of other people
I thought should be considered.

233
00:10:44,353 --> 00:10:45,396
Uh...

234
00:10:45,479 --> 00:10:47,398
If anybody's going to
Florida, it should be me.

235
00:10:47,481 --> 00:10:50,651
Every shirt that I have that isn't
a work shirt is a Tommy Bahama.

236
00:10:50,735 --> 00:10:53,404
I'm the only person in this
office who watches Burn Notice.

237
00:10:53,571 --> 00:10:56,657
Okay. I did not realize that so
many of you wanted to go so badly,

238
00:10:56,741 --> 00:10:59,827
so if you feel like you have a
very good reason to go to Florida,

239
00:10:59,910 --> 00:11:01,412
I'm happy to hear you out.
Great idea.

240
00:11:01,495 --> 00:11:03,497
So why doesn't everyone just
take the next five to six hours,

241
00:11:03,581 --> 00:11:05,958
come up with a statement on why you
feel you deserve to go to Florida,

242
00:11:06,042 --> 00:11:07,043
and Andy and I
will listen to it.

243
00:11:07,168 --> 00:11:09,795
No, that is a ridiculous waste of time.
For you maybe.

244
00:11:10,171 --> 00:11:11,714
Five to six hours?
Three to four hours.

245
00:11:11,797 --> 00:11:14,008
No,no,no,no.No.
Just two to three hours.

246
00:11:14,091 --> 00:11:16,510
Come up with a statement
over the next hour.

247
00:11:16,594 --> 00:11:17,595
Thirty minutes.

248
00:11:18,179 --> 00:11:19,430
I'm out.

249
00:11:19,597 --> 00:11:20,973
Okay, read it
back to me.

250
00:11:21,057 --> 00:11:23,517
"Robert, great offer. Wish I could
hit the links with you in Florida,

251
00:11:23,601 --> 00:11:26,771
"but a father of a newborn really should be
helping out his wife any chance he gets."

252
00:11:27,188 --> 00:11:30,399
Good. Doesn't sound pushy.
You're just stating a fact.

253
00:11:30,483 --> 00:11:32,443
Absolutely.
Works for me.

254
00:11:32,985 --> 00:11:35,071
Okay. Sending.

255
00:11:35,154 --> 00:11:37,573
Well done. And we managed
to kill the entire morning.

256
00:11:37,656 --> 00:11:39,283
We really did.

257
00:11:45,331 --> 00:11:46,957
He responded, "L.O.L."

258
00:11:55,549 --> 00:11:58,552
I have a new swimsuit I need to break in.
I am already...

259
00:11:58,636 --> 00:12:02,348
I already picked my team, Darryl,
Oscar, Angela, Phyllis and Toby.

260
00:12:02,515 --> 00:12:04,308
And that is the team
that I am taking.

261
00:12:04,725 --> 00:12:07,728
What's that? It's not up to me?
I only have influence?

262
00:12:07,812 --> 00:12:10,606
Well, that's all
Baltzer Glattfuelder had,

263
00:12:10,689 --> 00:12:13,359
and now no one eats
owls for Thanksgiving.

264
00:12:13,943 --> 00:12:16,028
You have two young, dynamic
people in this office

265
00:12:16,112 --> 00:12:17,822
who know trends,
who know youth.

266
00:12:17,905 --> 00:12:20,908
That's myself and Kelly Kapoor,
you need one of us there.

267
00:12:21,992 --> 00:12:24,829
Or both? Not both. Just one. Me.

268
00:12:24,912 --> 00:12:27,373
Or if not me, Kelly.
Ideally me.

269
00:12:27,456 --> 00:12:28,791
Again, youth knowledge,

270
00:12:28,874 --> 00:12:31,502
that's what you get when you put Ryan
Howard on your special project team.

271
00:12:32,002 --> 00:12:35,172
Or Kelly Kapoor.
Again, not both. Thanks.

272
00:12:36,507 --> 00:12:38,134
Very impressive.

273
00:12:38,259 --> 00:12:40,678
He put a lot of work into that.
Mm-hmm.

274
00:12:40,761 --> 00:12:42,763
PowerPoints are the peacocks
of the business world.

275
00:12:42,847 --> 00:12:44,390
All show, no meat.

276
00:12:45,015 --> 00:12:48,602
Because I feel like that I am in
a place with my gambling rehab

277
00:12:48,686 --> 00:12:52,022
that I can finally start
going to dog racing again.

278
00:12:52,648 --> 00:12:57,111
You know, just sitting,
watching, enjoying the sport.

279
00:12:57,194 --> 00:13:01,449
Maybe putting down a few dollars if there's
like a crazy mismatch or something.

280
00:13:01,532 --> 00:13:03,617
Okay. Thank you, Kevin,
we'll let you know.

281
00:13:03,701 --> 00:13:05,369
Thank you.
When do we leave?

282
00:13:05,661 --> 00:13:07,037
Thank you.

283
00:13:07,329 --> 00:13:10,374
It really has been
a long, lonely winter.

284
00:13:10,541 --> 00:13:14,420
Wow. Seasonal affective
disorder, a.k.a. SAD.

285
00:13:14,503 --> 00:13:16,881
That sounds like
a very real thing, Toby.

286
00:13:17,089 --> 00:13:19,717
It is real. Thank you
for saying that.

287
00:13:20,009 --> 00:13:22,595
Yeah, wow,
it's almost like

288
00:13:22,678 --> 00:13:26,640
we're not all experiencing
the same winter.

289
00:13:26,724 --> 00:13:30,394
Florida Stanley smiles. Florida
Stanley is happy to go to work.

290
00:13:30,478 --> 00:13:33,230
Florida Stanley is who you
want on your Florida team.

291
00:13:35,816 --> 00:13:37,943
It's still... Well.
...she does here.

292
00:13:38,027 --> 00:13:39,904
Hey, guys.
Any spots left?

293
00:13:40,279 --> 00:13:41,906
Erin, you want to go
to Tallahassee?

294
00:13:42,448 --> 00:13:43,991
I do. I really do.

295
00:13:44,074 --> 00:13:46,202
I think it would be a nice
way to clear my head.

296
00:13:47,495 --> 00:13:49,747
You know, I don't think it's a good
idea for you to clear your head

297
00:13:49,830 --> 00:13:51,665
any more than it
has been already.

298
00:13:51,749 --> 00:13:53,417
I think you need
a workplace where

299
00:13:53,501 --> 00:13:56,962
the burdens of everyday life
keep you tethered to reality.

300
00:13:57,421 --> 00:13:58,464
Either or.

301
00:13:58,547 --> 00:14:01,217
But if you can find someone to
fill in, I would love to go.

302
00:14:01,342 --> 00:14:03,427
Well, it wouldn't be hard
to find someone to fill in.

303
00:14:04,595 --> 00:14:07,806
Really? Oh, great. Good.

304
00:14:08,557 --> 00:14:11,101
Now, is it too dark to say that
Cece's having an operation?

305
00:14:11,685 --> 00:14:13,103
I think you need
to go to Florida.

306
00:14:14,021 --> 00:14:15,314
I think you're right.

307
00:14:15,397 --> 00:14:17,691
It's only for three weeks.

308
00:14:17,775 --> 00:14:19,860
You know, with my mom and sister
at the house, it'll be...

309
00:14:19,944 --> 00:14:20,986
A total nightmare.

310
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:24,073
I was going to say good, because
I'll have all the help I need.

311
00:14:24,156 --> 00:14:25,741
Yeah, that's what I'm saying.
They're incredibly helpful.

312
00:14:25,824 --> 00:14:27,076
You're lucky
to have them.

313
00:14:27,159 --> 00:14:28,744
With them there, you probably
won't even know I'm gone.

314
00:14:28,827 --> 00:14:30,079
Exactly.

315
00:14:32,790 --> 00:14:33,791
Well...

316
00:14:34,124 --> 00:14:39,129
No. No! Absolutely not. You are not going!
Over my dead body, no. Andy...

317
00:14:39,213 --> 00:14:41,423
Toons, I'm really sorry.

318
00:14:41,507 --> 00:14:43,717
You're too essential to the operation here.
I can't let you go.

319
00:14:43,801 --> 00:14:46,262
I wouldn't say that.
That's a bit much.

320
00:14:46,345 --> 00:14:47,680
You know, Dwight, if you
didn't want me to go,

321
00:14:47,763 --> 00:14:50,849
the smart move would be to tell Andy that
I actually am essential to the operation.

322
00:14:50,933 --> 00:14:52,101
That way, I couldn't go.

323
00:14:53,143 --> 00:14:55,396
Jim is essential... Hold on. I
just want to get it on camera.

324
00:14:57,314 --> 00:15:00,943
Andy, Jim is just too...

325
00:15:01,151 --> 00:15:02,152
Essential

326
00:15:02,778 --> 00:15:03,946
This is stupid! Cut!

327
00:15:04,154 --> 00:15:05,322
All right. I'm going
to pack my trunks.

328
00:15:05,406 --> 00:15:06,448
He doesn't even
want to go.

329
00:15:06,615 --> 00:15:07,950
Well, I got a text
from R.C.

330
00:15:08,033 --> 00:15:11,620
inviting me to come down, so I think
that's going to make us roommates.

331
00:15:12,830 --> 00:15:13,872
Oh, my God.

332
00:15:14,081 --> 00:15:17,084
Hey, quick question, do you shower at night?
Or do you shower in the morning?

333
00:15:17,167 --> 00:15:19,670
Because I want to shower when you're
showering to save some water.

334
00:15:22,089 --> 00:15:24,675
Okay, listen up, everyone.
Here's who's going to Florida.

335
00:15:25,217 --> 00:15:26,385
Cathy.
What?

336
00:15:26,468 --> 00:15:27,595
Stanley.
No.

337
00:15:27,678 --> 00:15:28,804
Ryan.
No!

338
00:15:28,887 --> 00:15:30,014
Erin.

339
00:15:30,097 --> 00:15:31,348
And Jim.

340
00:15:31,432 --> 00:15:33,684
You got to be
kidding me. Okay.

341
00:15:34,685 --> 00:15:36,020
Okay.

342
00:15:36,562 --> 00:15:38,856
Florida group,
welcome to the team.

343
00:15:48,365 --> 00:15:50,284
Let's go!

344
00:15:50,367 --> 00:15:53,621
Step it up, you runts.
You infants. Let's move.

345
00:15:53,704 --> 00:15:55,122
Why is it so
hot in here?

346
00:15:55,205 --> 00:15:59,251
Hot? What are you talking about? This
is a nice, temperate Florida morning.

347
00:15:59,335 --> 00:16:01,378
Eighty-five degrees,
73% humidity.

348
00:16:02,046 --> 00:16:04,006
This is the exact environment
that you will be facing

349
00:16:04,089 --> 00:16:06,133
for the next three
weeks of your life.

350
00:16:06,216 --> 00:16:09,637
Welcome to Special
Projects orientation.

351
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:11,764
The next three weeks
of your life

352
00:16:11,847 --> 00:16:15,225
are going to be the most
miserable you've ever faced.

353
00:16:15,309 --> 00:16:18,020
They're going to be hard.
They're going to be dirty.

354
00:16:18,103 --> 00:16:20,397
You're going to
wish you were dead.

355
00:16:21,315 --> 00:16:22,316
But?

356
00:16:22,399 --> 00:16:24,109
"But?" There's no "buts."

357
00:16:24,193 --> 00:16:27,071
That's it. You'll wish you were dead.
You seem a little disturbed.

358
00:16:27,196 --> 00:16:28,864
Do you want to stay here?
No. No. I want to go.

359
00:16:28,947 --> 00:16:32,159
Because if any of you
would prefer to stay here,

360
00:16:32,242 --> 00:16:34,370
all you need to do
is ring this bell.

361
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:36,246
Is that the buzzer
from Taboo?

362
00:16:36,914 --> 00:16:38,624
Shut up, maggot!
It is.

363
00:16:38,707 --> 00:16:40,376
You don't want to go,
ring the bell!

364
00:16:40,709 --> 00:16:42,419
Ring it. Ring it.

365
00:16:42,711 --> 00:16:45,756
How many of you have seen the
documentary Deliverance?

366
00:16:47,341 --> 00:16:48,967
How did a mosquito
get in here?

367
00:16:49,593 --> 00:16:51,845
I released 300 mosquitoes
into the conference room.

368
00:16:51,929 --> 00:16:53,180
Just temporarily.

369
00:16:53,263 --> 00:16:56,058
When I'm done, the frogs will
take care of the mosquitoes.

370
00:16:58,769 --> 00:17:02,481
Yes. And then he brushes
your soft supple cheeks

371
00:17:02,564 --> 00:17:05,818
with his worn leathery
hands and says,

372
00:17:05,901 --> 00:17:09,029
"I'm going to make you
the seventh Mrs. Rosenblat!"

373
00:17:09,363 --> 00:17:10,781
Unless you ring
the bell. Ring it.

374
00:17:10,864 --> 00:17:11,949
No.
Ring the bell.

375
00:17:12,032 --> 00:17:14,868
You want to spend the rest of your life
changing your husband's colostomy bags?

376
00:17:14,952 --> 00:17:17,287
Huh? Do you? Ring it!
What? Ah.

377
00:17:18,872 --> 00:17:20,290
What was that?
Mosquito.

378
00:17:27,047 --> 00:17:29,049
Orientation's over.

379
00:17:30,718 --> 00:17:32,469
Darryl, hey.

380
00:17:32,553 --> 00:17:33,637
Hey, what's up?
You called?

381
00:17:33,887 --> 00:17:35,931
Yeah, you know,
I felt really terrible

382
00:17:36,014 --> 00:17:38,475
that you got me these lovely gloves,
and I didn't get you anything.

383
00:17:38,559 --> 00:17:39,601
It's cool, really.

384
00:17:39,685 --> 00:17:41,520
No, it's anything
but cool

385
00:17:41,603 --> 00:17:46,734
Now, I haven't really had time to
go out and buy you anything, but

386
00:17:46,817 --> 00:17:48,235
here goes.

387
00:17:48,318 --> 00:17:51,947
"This coupon entitles you to one
free tickle monster attack."

388
00:17:52,030 --> 00:17:55,534
Yeah. They're Nate coupons or Nate-pons.
And they're all different.

389
00:17:55,784 --> 00:17:58,370
Cash that one in and I will
bring you a stick of gum

390
00:17:58,871 --> 00:18:01,915
any time, any place.
I'll find you.

391
00:18:03,041 --> 00:18:04,293
Thanks.

392
00:18:06,211 --> 00:18:07,254
Nice flowers.

393
00:18:07,337 --> 00:18:08,881
Thanks.They're
from my mom.

394
00:18:10,340 --> 00:18:11,759
Your mom?

395
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:14,094
Yeah.

396
00:18:14,178 --> 00:18:15,387
Oh.

397
00:18:15,471 --> 00:18:19,224
So I guess that was your mom who called
me earlier looking for the address.

398
00:18:19,308 --> 00:18:20,517
Real deep-voiced woman.

399
00:18:21,059 --> 00:18:22,186
Yeah, that's her.

400
00:18:22,269 --> 00:18:25,189
She said her name
was Brandon, I think.

401
00:18:25,272 --> 00:18:26,815
Your mom's name
is Brandon?

402
00:18:28,734 --> 00:18:31,987
Yeah, Darryl. My mom's
name is Brandon.

403
00:18:35,866 --> 00:18:38,911
I guess I figured
out where I stand.

404
00:18:38,994 --> 00:18:42,039
This is a love beanie.

405
00:18:45,542 --> 00:18:48,295
Okay, Florida team,
let's reconvene.

406
00:18:59,097 --> 00:19:02,392
I'm going to Florida.
And I'm not coming back.

407
00:19:05,354 --> 00:19:08,816
So Sabre has set up a conference room
for our use while we're at corporate.

408
00:19:08,899 --> 00:19:10,734
So what is this
special project?

409
00:19:10,901 --> 00:19:12,528
Basically, Sabre has
tasked us with

410
00:19:12,611 --> 00:19:14,530
helping them branch into
the retail market place.

411
00:19:14,613 --> 00:19:16,406
They want to set up their own
version of an Apple store.

412
00:19:16,698 --> 00:19:17,741
That sounds awesome.

413
00:19:17,991 --> 00:19:19,701
It did. It did.

414
00:19:19,785 --> 00:19:20,828
You know what
might be great?

415
00:19:20,911 --> 00:19:23,705
Is if instead of a genius
bar, we have a lounge area

416
00:19:23,789 --> 00:19:25,999
where people can ask
questions about the product

417
00:19:26,083 --> 00:19:27,584
with other customers
who liked it.

418
00:19:28,418 --> 00:19:31,588
Yeah, and they should have a place where
people can check their computers or printers

419
00:19:31,672 --> 00:19:34,132
before they see you, so you don't
have to carry it around like a dope.

420
00:19:34,675 --> 00:19:35,759
Like a coat check.

421
00:19:41,348 --> 00:19:42,683
Oh, don't write it down
unless you like it.

422
00:19:42,766 --> 00:19:43,767
Oh, I like it.

423
00:19:44,351 --> 00:19:46,854
It seems to me that the Apple
store is kind of like a party,

424
00:19:46,937 --> 00:19:49,773
so I think our question is, how
do we make this a better party?

425
00:19:49,857 --> 00:19:53,527
No. We sell business tools and
the stores need to reflect that.

426
00:19:53,610 --> 00:19:56,947
They need to be all business. Let all
the other stores look like a toy store.

427
00:19:57,072 --> 00:20:00,367
Right. Think different.
From Apple.

428
00:20:03,203 --> 00:20:05,289
Dwight, is now a good time to
go over what you expect of us?

429
00:20:12,921 --> 00:20:14,423
Yes.

430
00:20:14,506 --> 00:20:16,341
What are my expectations
for the group?

431
00:20:16,466 --> 00:20:19,887
I have been given the responsibility
to manage Stanley, a solid player.

432
00:20:19,970 --> 00:20:22,097
Ryan, who is capable
of surprises.

433
00:20:22,347 --> 00:20:24,016
Erin, an excellent
follower.

434
00:20:24,433 --> 00:20:27,477
And Cathy, a probably not
totally useless enigma.

435
00:20:27,603 --> 00:20:29,980
And, well, Jim.

436
00:20:30,898 --> 00:20:34,318
Under the right manager,
that's not a bad team.

437
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:37,154
Perfektenschlag.

438
00:20:44,036 --> 00:20:45,495
I don't know how I'm going
to live here without you.

439
00:20:45,787 --> 00:20:48,248
Me neither. Hey, will you
put this coat on my chair?

440
00:20:48,332 --> 00:20:49,666
I just realized I'm not going
to need it down there.

441
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:51,376
Yes, I will take
your coat.

442
00:20:51,460 --> 00:20:52,794
And I will keep it
with me,

443
00:20:52,878 --> 00:20:54,588
and I will sleep with it
because it smells like you.

444
00:20:54,671 --> 00:20:56,006
No,no,no,no,no.
Just put it on my chair.

445
00:20:58,008 --> 00:20:59,217
All right.
Bye.

446
00:20:59,301 --> 00:21:01,720
Bye. Call me when you land.
I will.

447
00:21:02,721 --> 00:21:04,014
Safe travels!

448
00:21:04,389 --> 00:21:07,184
Goodbye for a very,
very long time.

449
00:21:08,268 --> 00:21:09,353
Okay.

450
00:21:09,811 --> 00:21:11,188
It's only three weeks.

451
00:21:15,776 --> 00:21:17,194
All expenses paid.

452
00:21:18,278 --> 00:21:19,738
Yeah. Jim's going
to be there.

453
00:21:19,821 --> 00:21:23,200
That marriage is not good.
Nobody knows better than me.

454
00:21:24,493 --> 00:21:26,036
Definitely, we will.

455
00:21:26,119 --> 00:21:29,331
It's three weeks in Tallahassee.
What else is there to do?

